ture and  was soon developed by a legendary  scientist james hansen and particularly  in a 2008 paper by tim lenton at al  incidentally how we use the phrase  tipping point in climate science has  quite an interesting history i'll leave  a link to a paper below if you'd like to  learn more suffice to say i'm  simplifying things for this video lenten  ettel describes tipping elements parts  of the earth system which could with  just a nudge be changed from one state  to another and would have a large effect  on global climate if they did so the  point at which each tipping element  changes from one state to another is the  tipping point and each point is specific  to each element each tipping element  then is like an exposed wire in the  bucket and the tipping point is how high  each wire sits above the water line the  2008 paper highlighted nine tipping  elements which could impact world  climate perhaps the best known of these  is north atlantic deep water formation  which drives the thermo haline  circulation the big overturning  circulation of water in the atlantic  ocean if sufficient melt water entered  the north atlantic from greenland this  deep water formation could be disrupted  and the overturning circulation switched  off if this were to happen the northern  hemisphere could cool by several degrees  celsius and there could be a return of  ice age conditions to europe this effect  was dramatized in the film the day after  tomorrow now in our analogy this is like  a wire short circuiting not adding an  extra tap to the bucket but drilling an  extra hole in the bucket which is  incidentally what i wished had happened  to my head whilst i was watching the day  after tomorrow however the lentinet l  paper cites the ipcc assessment that  this was quote very unlikely to happen  before the year 2100 at least in a rapid  manner it was believed that climatic  tipping points could be reached this  century particularly in arctic sea ice  melting and melting of the greenland ice  sheet but that this was not likely  however to quote the paper given the  large uncertainty that remains about  tipping elements there is an urgent need  to improve our understanding of the  underlying physical mechanisms  determining their behavior so that  policymakers are able  to avoid the unmanageable and manage the  unavoidable in other words we knew they  were wise in the bucket but they were  quite high above the rising water line  we didn't know exactly where but we had  no reason to believe we would hit them  very soon however that was in 2008. this  month a new paper came out which counted  amongst its authors tim lenten now at  the university of exeter where i did my  phd it had the incredibly sobering title  exceeding 1.5 degrees c global warming  could trigger multiple climate tipping  points the authors reviewed over 200  peer-reviewed papers published since  2008 to assess our state of knowledge of  tipping points in the climate system  through paleoclimate data through modern  observations and through the output of  computer models the result they  increased the number of potential  tipping elements in the climate system  from 9 to 16. but that wasn't the  shocking part  the shocking part was that they  concluded we have already warmed the  planet to the point  where five of those tipping elements  could be triggered  now  the wires may already be in the water  just  waiting to short circuit collapse of the  greenland and west antarctic ice sheets  widespread abrupt permafrost thaw  collapse of convection in the labrador  sea and massive die-off of tropical  coral reefs are already possible at one  degree of warming that we have already  caused since pre-industrial times and to  be crystal clear we don't think any of  these tipping points have been reached  yet  but if they are reached there may be no  going back the changes may be  self-sustaining  to quote lenten that means even if  temperature stops rising once the ice  sheet ocean o